---
# HOW TO EDIT THE REAL FILE
#
# secret.sops.yaml already exists next to this template and is already
# encrypted, so the normal path is to edit it in place:
#
#   SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=age.agekey sops <dir>/secret.sops.yaml
#
# That opens the DECRYPTED contents in $EDITOR and re-encrypts on save.
#
# Do NOT run `sops --encrypt --in-place` on it. sops refuses with exit 203
# ("the file you have provided contains a top-level entry called 'sops' ...
# generally due to the file already being encrypted"). Harmless, but it is
# not the command you want.
#
# Recreating it from this template instead:
#
#   cp secret.sops.yaml.example secret.sops.yaml
#   $EDITOR secret.sops.yaml                    # fill in real values
#   sops --encrypt --in-place secret.sops.yaml  # only on a PLAINTEXT file
#
# .sops.yaml restricts encryption to the `data`/`stringData` keys, so the
# structure stays readable in Git and only the values are ciphertext.
# The unencrypted file must never be committed — see gitops/README.md.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: n8n-secrets
  namespace: ai-system
type: Opaque
stringData:
  # The key name is fixed by the chart: `existingEncryptionKeySecret` looks
  # for exactly N8N_ENCRYPTION_KEY. Generate it, do not invent one:
  #   openssl rand -hex 32
  #
  # BACK THIS UP SOMEWHERE OTHER THAN THIS REPO AND OTHER THAN THE PVC.
  # It decrypts every credential n8n stores. A database backup taken without
  # it restores a table of ciphertext nobody can read. Unlike WEBUI_SECRET_KEY
  # this cannot be rotated casually: changing it orphans every saved
  # credential, which must then be deleted and re-entered by hand.
  N8N_ENCRYPTION_KEY: "REPLACE-ME"
